text:
jane.cust: Hey team — escalated from billing: customers reporting duplicate POSTs (orders) after a retry. Happened when network flapped and the SDK retried automatically. Node 18 app, undici 5.x, @redwood/sdk 1.11.0. Repro snippet attached below.

jane.cust: ```ts
// simplified repro using the app's retry wrapper
const ac = new AbortController(); // <-- created once at top-level
async function sendOrder(body) {
  return sdk.fetch('/orders', {
    method: 'POST',
    body: JSON.stringify(body),
    headers: { 'content-type': 'application/json' },
    signal: ac.signal,
  });
}
// network blip causes retry, we see duplicate orders
```

maya.dev: thx for the report — can you paste exact versions (node, undici, sdk) and any server logs? quick guess: AbortSignal being reused across retry attempts could let retry re-send while original signal already aborted.

jane.cust: node=v18.18.0, undici@5.10.0, @redwood/sdk@1.11.0. server logs show two POSTs ~500ms apart, both hit payment gateway. no Idempotency-Key present. no streaming body (we stringify).

ryan.k: since body is a plain string it's replayable, but reusing the same AbortController can cause the first attempt to abort mid-flight and the retry to start while the gateway still processes the original. undici's fetch + AbortSignal semantics: if you call controller.abort(), any in-flight body read may terminate but server may have accepted it. Are you using a shared controller for request-level timeouts?

jane.cust: yes we attach a global timeout controller from our request middleware so every outgoing call gets signal from same controller. wanted centralized timeout.

maya.dev: that's the root cause. quick fixes: 1) create a fresh AbortController for each attempt, or 2) include an Idempotency-Key header on unsafe endpoints so backend can dedupe. Example change:

maya.dev: ```ts
// per-attempt controller inside retry loop
async function sendOrder(body) {
  return retry(async () => {
    const attemptCtrl = new AbortController();
    const mergedSignal = mergeSignals(attemptCtrl.signal, requestLevelSignal); // or just use attemptCtrl
    return sdk.fetch('/orders', { method: 'POST', body: JSON.stringify(body), signal: mergedSignal });
  });
}
```

alex.support: I reproduced locally by simulating timeout and failure; I can confirm double-charge. Workaround for now: set 'Idempotency-Key' per order (UUID) and reduce automatic retries for POSTs. Also adding a temporary SDK override in our client config: 

alex.support: ```json
{"retry": {"maxRetries": 1, "retryOn": ["5xx","429"], "reuseAbortSignal": false}}
```

alex.support: I've opened a patch: https://git.internal/redwood/sdk/pull/482 that changes the internal retry to create a fresh AbortController per attempt and avoids reusing the original request signal. It landed in branch fix/retry-signal-isolation.

ops-bot: deploy: canary 1.12.2-rc1 deployed to staging :rocket: (includes fix/retry-signal-isolation)

jane.cust: thanks — any ETA on canary->prod? billing already saw a handful of duplicates today.

maya.dev: we're pushing canary test now; if no regressions we'll roll to prod tonight UTC. For immediate mitigation enable Idempotency-Key and reduce POST retries. Sorry for the hit, we'll follow up with postmortem and billing remediation guidance. :pray:
